  <p>Nearly all of it traces back to one thing: the MEP coordination was based on drawings that didn't match the building. On a retrofit, the existing-conditions scan is what prevents that. It's often the single highest-value scan on the whole project.</p>

  <h2>Why existing buildings are the hard case</h2>

  <p>In new construction, MEP systems are designed into empty space and coordinated in a model before anything is installed. In a retrofit, you're threading new systems through a building that's already full, full of structure, full of decades of previous mechanical, electrical, and plumbing work, much of it undocumented or documented incorrectly.</p>

  <p>The original drawings, if they exist, show design intent from years ago. They don't show the duct that was rerouted in 2009, the abandoned conduit nobody removed, or the fact that the "clear" plenum is actually 4 inches shorter than drawn. Every one of those discrepancies is a coordination problem waiting to surface during installation, the worst possible time to find it.</p>



